history_summary_node.summary_text:
# Conversation Summary

## 1. Previous Conversation

The user requested replication of a specific webpage (https://axtira.webflow.io/home-one) with accurate layout and design. The AI successfully created a full replica using Vite, vanilla HTML/CSS, and JavaScript, including all major sections. The dev server was launched and is running on http://localhost:5173.

Throughout the conversation, the user requested several design iterations:
- Converting all colors to OKLCH color space
- Replacing the original black color (#15151f) with OKLCH equivalent of #06559f (blue)
- Creating complementary darker blue for section backgrounds with gradient
- Replacing all gold colors with OKLCH equivalent of #16dbe1 (cyan)
- Replacing "Axtira" text logo with `/altius-lifts.svg` image at 50px height
- Replacing warm cream background with ice-blue tint at 93% lightness
- Replacing emoji icons with Font Awesome icons
- Creating cobalt blue variable for H2 headings on light sections
- Restructuring hero to use full-width background image with overlay
- Adding hero image at `/hero1.webp` with grayscale filter
- Creating darker overlay variable for hero
- Aligning hero content to the left
- Integrating content-grid layout system (Kevin Powell / Ryan Mulligan pattern)
- Integrating Alpine.js with scroll animations using x-intersect plugin
- Reducing border-radius on all rounded buttons from 50px to var(--radius) (12px)
- Adding grayscale filter to hero background image

The user then made significant content changes behind the scenes, converting the energy/oil & gas site to a lift company (Altius Lifts) with real content about lift installation, maintenance, modernization, and repairs.

The user then requested renaming class names in the HTML to be more relevant to the subject of each element and adjusting the CSS accordingly. The AI completed this task, renaming:
- About section: `cta-partner-*` → `about-*`
- Service Details section: `innovation-*` → `detail-*`, `id="innovation"` → `id="service-details"`
- Sectors section: `project-*` → `sector-*`, `id="projects"` → `id="sectors"`

Following this, the user requested several display fixes:
1. Fixed sectors section cards to display in 2 columns on desktop and ensured image fills parent
2. Adjusted `.sectors-header` text width to align with card column boundary
3. Added dark semi-transparent container to `.sector-main-content` for text readability
4. Left-aligned `.detail-header` h2 title and added `breakout` class for proper alignment
5. Changed `.services-grid` from 3 columns to 2 columns
6. Updated service card icons to be more appropriate (elevator, wand-magic-sparkles, clipboard-check, wrench)

## 2. Current Work

The user identified that the `.service-details` section cards were poorly designed compared to other sections and proposed a major redesign. After discussion, the user referenced the Axtira website's carousel pattern in their `.rt-resource-content-main-wrapper` section and asked if Alpine.js could handle a similar tabbed carousel implementation.

The AI analyzed the Axtira site and proposed a tabbed carousel design that would:
- Replace both `#services` and `#service-details` sections with a single component
- Use Alpine.js for state management and transitions
- Feature tab buttons with icons at the top
- Display one content panel at a time with image, title, description, check-marked list items, and CTA
- Use dark blue background matching the existing design system
- Eliminate card overload by focusing on one service at a time

The user approved this approach and the AI successfully implemented the tabbed service carousel with:
- Single `#services` section replacing both old sections
- Section header with white H2 on dark blue gradient
