history_summary_node.summary_text:
# Conversation Summary

## 1. Previous Conversation

The user requested replication of a specific webpage (https://axtira.webflow.io/home-one) with accurate layout and design. The AI successfully created a full replica using Vite, vanilla HTML/CSS, and JavaScript, including all major sections. The dev server was launched and is running on http://localhost:5173.

Throughout the conversation, the user requested several design iterations:
- Converting all colors to OKLCH color space
- Replacing the original black color (#15151f) with OKLCH equivalent of #06559f (blue)
- Creating complementary darker blue for section backgrounds with gradient
- Replacing all gold colors with OKLCH equivalent of #16dbe1 (cyan)
- Replacing "Axtira" text logo with `/altius-lifts.svg` image at 50px height
- Replacing warm cream background with ice-blue tint at 93% lightness
- Replacing emoji icons with Font Awesome icons
- Creating cobalt blue variable for H2 headings on light sections
- Restructuring hero to use full-width background image with overlay
- Adding hero image at `/hero1.webp` with grayscale filter
- Creating darker overlay variable for hero
- Aligning hero content to the left
- Integrating content-grid layout system (Kevin Powell / Ryan Mulligan pattern)
- Integrating Alpine.js with scroll animations using x-intersect plugin
- Reducing border-radius on all rounded buttons from 50px to var(--radius) (12px)
- Adding grayscale filter to hero background image

The user then made significant content changes behind the scenes, converting the energy/oil & gas site to a lift company (Altius Lifts) with real content about lift installation, maintenance, modernization, and repairs.

The user then requested renaming class names in the HTML to be more relevant to the subject of each element and adjusting the CSS accordingly. The AI completed this task, renaming various sections.

Following this, the user requested several display fixes and design improvements including fixing sectors section cards, adjusting text widths, adding dark containers, changing grid layouts, updating icons, and redesigning the services section as a tabbed carousel.

The user then requested various refinements including changing CTAs to text links, adding FAQ section with accordions, styling improvements, adding section tags/badges, replacing arrow icons with Font Awesome, and moving sector card numbers to top-right corners.

The user then requested decorative elements to break up the page. Initially, blueprint-style connectors were attempted but rejected. The final solution was simple vertical white lines positioned at 80% across the page (alternating to 20% on even dividers), with responsive height scaling.

Additional improvements included:
- Adding gradient to navbar on scroll
- Redesigning footer with 4 columns (logo/brand, Quick Links, Opening Hours, Contact Us)
- Adding gradient underlines to footer headings
- Adding gradients to service tab backgrounds
- Implementing auto-rotation for service tabs (20s delay, then 10s intervals)
- Animating the lists in the services section with staggered entrance effects
- Fixing auto-rotation timer issues with proper cleanup

The user acknowledged that the page was "vibe coded" and requested a full audit to identify unused or overbloated code before rolling the design out to other website pages. The AI completed a comprehensive audit and presented findings in 4 categories. The user approved with "proceed" and the AI completed the full cleanup in 7 phases, removing 272 lines of bloat (16% reduction).

After the cleanup, the user requested more vertical space at the top and bottom of each section, and more space at the top of the footer. The AI increased `--section-pad` from 100px to 120px, then to 140px, footer top padding from 64px to 96px then to 120px, and mobile section padding from 64px to 80px then to 100px.

The user then identified several responsive issues that needed addressing, which the AI successfully completed:
1. Content-grid padding, nav mobile breakpoint, hero CTAs stacking, service tabs overflow, sectors layout, footer layout, and horizontal overflow fixes
2. Hero buttons stacking below 460px and becoming full width when stacked
3. Removing bottom margin from `.hero-ctas`
4. Increasing hero bottom padding on large screens and decreasing top padding at 768px
5. Fixing alignment issues between topbar/navbar and the rest of the page content

The user questioned whether the width of the site and immediate elements should be controlled only by the content grid. The AI confirmed this is correct and proposed two options. The user chose **Option 1** - making content-grid the single source of truth. The AI completed a comprehensive refactor making `#topbar`, `#navbar`, and `#hero` use content-grid with breakout children, removing all custom width systems.

Following this, the user requested that in the sectors section, the `.sector-main` element should switch to a fixed 60% height at 960px instead of stretching to match the sidebar depth. The AI implemented this with proper responsive behavior.

The user then requested swapping the positions of `.sector-main` and `.sector-sidebar` at 768px using CSS `order` property.

The user observed that the FAQ section tag, title, and subtext were the only elements of this nature on the page that did not align left as the screen width narrows. The AI fixed this by removing `text-align: center` from `.faq-header` and `margin: 0 auto` from `.faq-list`.

The user then requested adding an image to the FAQ section (`/lift-accessability.jpg`) to fill the space on the right for desktops. The AI implemented a 2-column layout with the FAQ content on the left and image on the right, stacking to single column at ≤768px.

However, the FAQ accordion items disappeared from the page. The AI identified that the animation selectors only went 2 levels deep but the FAQ items were now 3 levels deep inside the new wrapper. The fix was to switch from strict child combinators to descendant selectors (`.in-view .anim-fade-up` instead of `.in-view > * > .anim-fade-up`).

The user then requested that the FAQ image should not take up a full half of the screen and should not be affected by the varying height of the FAQ accordion. The AI changed the grid from `1fr 1fr` to `1fr 420px` and gave the image a fixed height of 420px.

The user requested the image be slightly wider (increased to 420px width) and that the FAQ image top edge be aligned with the top of the section's h2. The AI added `margin-top: 59px` to `.faq-img` to align with the h2 (accounting for the section tag height + margin).

## 2. Current Work

The user committed and pushed all changes to GitHub, then asked if the AI wanted to conduct a thorough audit and look for potential improvements and optimizations, specifically asking what could be changed to maintain DRY without affecting the overall structure and design.

The AI conducted a comprehensive audit of `index.html`, `style.css`, and `main.js`, identifying issues in 4 categories:

**1. Dead Code (safe to remove):**
- `--gray-100` variable (never referenced)
- `--radius-lg` variable (never referenced)
- `gap: 0` in `.faq-list` and `.sc-panel` (default values)
- `color: var(--white)` in `.sc-tab.active` (identical to base)
- `.sc-tab span { display: inline; }` at 600px (already inline by default)
- `.footer-brand { grid-column: 1 / -1; }` at 960px (already inherited from 1024px)
- `<!-- CTA SECTION -->` orphan comment
- Extra blank lines

**2. Redundancies (can be merged):**
- Duplicate `.sector-sidebar` at 768px (two separate rules)
- `.hero-badge` vs `.section-tag-dark` (near-identical styles)

**3. DRY Opportunities (extract shared patterns):**
- Section heading h2 pattern (used by 4 different sections)
- Cover image pattern (`width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover;`)
- Hero overlay gradient (hardcoded `oklch(15% 0.12 253.58)` 3 times)

**4. Keep (verified as needed):**
- All 14 `--white-*` alpha variables
- `--accent-light`, `--dark-mid`
- `font-family: inherit` on buttons
- `overflow-x: hidden` on both html and body

