There it is. Now I have the full picture. Here's how the page transition on that site works:

---

## Page Transition Breakdown

There are **two separate mechanisms** working together:

### 1. Initial Page Load — Visibility Toggle (FOUC prevention)

This is the simpler part — identical to what we just fixed on your site:

**Inline CSS in `<head>`:**
```css
body { visibility: hidden; }
body.loaded { visibility: visible; }
```

**JavaScript (two redundant triggers for safety):**
```js
// app.js — adds loading states in sequence
document.documentElement.classList.add("loading");

// On DOMContentLoaded:
document.documentElement.classList.remove("loading");
document.documentElement.classList.add("loaded");
setTimeout(() => document.documentElement.classList.add("transitions-enabled"), 100);

// On window load:
document.documentElement.classList.add("fully-loaded");
setTimeout(() => document.documentElement.classList.add("animations-enabled"), 200);

// Inline fallback at bottom of <body>:
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    setTimeout(() => document.body.classList.add('loaded'), 50);
});
setTimeout(() => document.body.classList.add('loaded'), 500); // hard fallback
```

This creates a **staggered reveal**: loading → loaded → transitions-enabled → fully-loaded → animations-enabled. The body stays `visibility: hidden` until `loaded` is applied, preventing FOUC.

### 2. Page-to-Page Transitions — CSS View Transitions API

This is the actual **between-page** transition. Two lines make it work:

**Meta tag in `<head>`:**
```html
<meta name="view-transition" content="same-origin">
```

**CSS rule:**
```css
@view-transition { navigation: auto; }
```

That's it. This is the **CSS View Transitions API (Level 2)** — a native browser feature (no JavaScript library needed). When you click any internal link, the browser automatically:

1. Takes a screenshot of the old page
2. Navigates to the new page
3. Cross-fades the old screenshot into the new page

The `same-origin` meta tag tells the browser to apply view transitions for all same-origin navigations. The `@view-transition { navigation: auto; }` CSS rule activates it. The default transition is a smooth **cross-fade** (~250ms).

---

### Want me to add this to our site?

It would be two additions:
1. A `<meta name="view-transition" content="same-origin">` tag in `<head>`
2. A `@view-transition { navigation: auto; }` rule in `base.css`

This would give you the same smooth cross-fade between pages once you build page 2. It's supported in Chrome, Edge, and Safari 18+ (Firefox is working on it — it falls back to a normal navigation gracefully).

